worked_examples.solution:
(a) Aluminium first, then iron, then glass, with wood last: the two metal rods lose their pins well before the two non-metal rods (aluminium is a better conductor than iron, and glass conducts slightly better than wood).

(b) The metal rods conduct energy faster because, in addition to the particle-to-particle vibration mechanism present in all four rods, they have mobile delocalised electrons that drift quickly from the hot end, carrying energy with them;

glass and wood have no such mobile electrons and must rely on the much slower particle-to-particle mechanism alone.

theory_blocks.body:
To describe a convection current fully, trace the loop in four stages, in order: **(1)** fluid near a heat source warms up and expands, becoming less dense;

**(2)** the surrounding denser, cooler fluid sinks and pushes the less dense fluid upward, away from the heat source; **(3)** as the risen fluid moves away from the heat source it gradually cools, becomes denser again, and sinks back down;

**(4)** the sunk fluid flows back toward the heat source to be warmed again, completing the loop.

A correct description names all four stages, not just 'warm fluid rises': without stages (3) and (4), the loop cannot actually be a continuous current, since fluid cannot pile up forever at the top without something bringing it back round.

