worked_examples.solution:
A low-placed radiator heats air near the floor, which expands, becomes less dense, and is pushed upward by denser surrounding air;

this rising air spreads near the ceiling, cools, sinks down the far side of the room, and flows back along the floor to the radiator, actively circulating warm air through the whole room via a full convection loop.

A radiator placed high up would not set up this full loop as effectively, since there is little room, and little denser air, above it to drive a strong upward push, leaving much of the room reliant on slower processes to warm up.

theory_blocks.body:
To compare how well two surfaces absorb (or emit) radiation fairly, every other variable that could affect the result must be controlled, changing only the surface finish being tested.

For an absorption comparison: use two containers of identical size and shape, holding equal masses of the same liquid, starting at the same temperature, placed at the same distance from the same radiant heat source, for the same length of time;

only the outer surface finish (for example, matte black versus shiny silver) should differ. Because the masses of liquid are equal, the bigger temperature rise shows which surface absorbed more radiation energy;

if you know specific heat capacity (extension, not required by this course), $Q=mc\Delta T$ turns each rise into an energy value.

For an emission comparison, the method is similar but reversed: both containers are heated to the same starting temperature,

then left to cool under identical conditions (same surroundings temperature, same distance from any surfaces, no draughts), and the surface that cools faster is the better emitter.

